# Intuition
<!-- Describe your first thoughts on how to solve this problem. -->
The problem requires us to generate a square matrix of size n with elements from 1 to n^2 in spiral order. To generate the matrix in spiral order, we can start by filling in the top row from left to right, then the right column from top to bottom, then the bottom row from right to left, and finally the left column from bottom to top. We can repeat this process until all the elements of the matrix have been filled in.
# Approach
<!-- Describe your approach to solving the problem. -->
1.Create an n x n matrix with all elements initialized to 0.
2.Initialize variables startingRow, endingRow, startingCol, and endingCol to keep track of the current boundaries of the matrix.
3.Initialize k to 1, which represents the current element to be filled in the matrix.
4.Use a while loop to iterate over the matrix until all elements have been filled.
5.Within the while loop, use four for loops to fill in the elements of the matrix in spiral order:
6.The first loop fills in the top row of the matrix from left to right.
7.The second loop fills in the right column of the matrix from top to bottom.
8.The third loop fills in the bottom row of the matrix from right to left.
9.The fourth loop fills in the left column of the matrix from bottom to top.
10.After each loop, update the boundaries of the matrix by incrementing or decrementing the appropriate variables.
11.Increment k after each element is filled in the matrix.
12.Return the resulting matrix.

# Complexity
- Time complexity:
<!-- Add your time complexity here, e.g. $$O(n)$$ -->
The time complexity of the given code is O(n^2) because the while loop iterates over all n^2 elements of the matrix, and each element is filled in exactly once.


- Space complexity:
<!-- Add your space complexity here, e.g. $$O(n)$$ -->
The space complexity of the given code is O(n^2) because we create an n x n matrix to store the resulting elements.

# Code
