# Approach
<!-- Describe your approach to solving the problem. -->
The solution involves a simple **iteration of the array**. 

*At each iteration* we have to check if **the point is valid**, in this case we have to check if the distance of this point is shorter than the distance that we found in previous iterations.

**Note:** The initialization of sIndex and sDistance is higher than the *maximum value allowed by the input conditions*.

# Complexity
- **Time complexity:** $O(n)$
- **Speed Beats:** 100%
<!-- Add your time complexity here, e.g. $$O(n)$$ -->

- **Space complexity:** $O(1)$
- **Memory Beats:** 78.3%
<!-- Add your space complexity here, e.g. $$O(n)$$ -->

# Code
```
class Solution {
    public int nearestValidPoint(int x, int y, int[][] points) {

        int sIndex = 100000;
        int sDistance = 100000;

        for (int i = 0; i < points.length; i++) {

            // Check if is a valid point
            if (points[i][0] == x || points[i][1] == y) {

                // Check if it is better than the previous best
                int distance = Math.abs(x - points[i][0]) + Math.abs(y - points[i][1]);
                if (sDistance > distance) {
                    sDistance = distance;
                    sIndex = i;
                }
            }
        }

        // Check if we have a valid point to return
        if (sIndex == 100000)
            return -1;
        return sIndex;
    }
}
